    "narrative": "The strongest play on the board for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who boasts an elite 77.67 efficiency score against Lodolo. Despite a slight penalty for being a left-on-left matchup, **Corbin Carroll** thrives due to the favorable 4.68 xERA of the opposing starter and an optimal #2 spot in the order that maximizes his stolen base upside\u2014a critical priority given our league's scarcity weighting. Similarly, **Gabriel Moreno** benefits from that same favorable matchup, with his 65.16 score bolstered by a strong xwOBA and a prime opportunity to drive in runs from the #3 hole. **Isaac Paredes** rounds out our high-leverage group at 62.38; he carries a significant platoon advantage against Ginn, which effectively offsets his slight park penalty, making him a reliable cornerstone for today\u2019s offensive output.\n\nOur toughest bench decision involved **Cole Young**, who fell just short of the starting cut with a 44.33 score. While **Cole Young** remains a high-value asset, particularly when considering his 62% stolen base potential\u2014an elite trait for our 5x5 needs\u2014the optimizer favored the efficiency of **Mickey Moniak** and **Jose Caballero**. While **Jose Caballero** faces a tough arm in Fluharty, his 13% platoon boost and sprint speed profile secured his spot, whereas **Cole Young** was squeezed out due to a defensive-heavy lineup shuffle. We are also closely monitoring the lineup card for **Seiya Suzuki**; though we have projected him in the #2 spot with a solid 52.78 efficiency, any deviation in batting order or a late-scratch scenario could necessitate a pivot to our bench depth. We continue to manage our remaining roster, including **Austin Riley**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte**, while navigating the availability of our IL-stashed contributors **James Wood** and **Trevor Story**.",
